ce":"gtwo_kml_202610202334","idp_filedate":1792539279000,"nhcTitle":"Offshore the East Coast of the United States (AL93)","discussion":"A storm-force non-tropical low pressure area located a few hundred  miles off the North Carolina coast is producing some shower and  thunderstorm activity primarily west of its center. Recent satellite  wind data indicated the low is still frontal. Over the next couple  of days, the low could develop some showers and thunderstorms near  the low-level center and gradually lose its frontal features while  it moves slowly north-northeastward over the Gulf Stream, allowing  the system to briefly develop subtropical characteristics. By the  end of the week, the system is expected to accelerate northeastward  away from the the eastern United States, where environmental  conditions are expected to become unfavorable for additional  subtropical or tropical development.  1. Regardless of the status of the system, high winds, coastal  flooding, and dangerous surf are expected along portions of the U.S.  East Coast during the next few days. For more information, please  consult statements and warnings from local National Weather Service  offices and the Weather Prediction Center, along with High Seas  Forecasts and Storm Warnings issued by the National Weather Service. \n Formation chance through 48 hours...medium...40 percent. \n Formation chance through 7 days...medium...40 percent.","disturbance":1}},{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"Polygon","coordinates":[[[-98.9885561240903,7.623666646375682],[-98.8935425892295,7.637144861546924],[-98.80122092712494,7.651000020888712],[-98.711508096223,7.665291958372437],[-98.62432105496994,7.680080507969493],[-98.53957676181209,7.695425503651274],[-98.4571921751957,7.711386779389168],[-98.37708425356713,7.728024169154574],[-98.29916995537268,7.74539750691888],[-98.22336623905859,7.76356662665348],[-98.14959006307124,7.782591362329767],[-98.0777583858569,7.802531547919133],[-98.00778816586188,7.82344